 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
 /*
- * Compile-only groundwork for the Apple T7000-family PCIe host controller.
+ * Apple T7000-family PCIe -- enable-sequence + generic-enumeration +
+ * PERST test.
  *
- * The real J81 ADT describes four ports, four port interrupts and twelve
- * register windows. Corellium's older H9P driver confirms the four-port
- * controller shape, but its register layout, SART and hard-coded PHY sequence
- * are not valid for T7000. The disabled device tree preserves J81's exact
- * resource ordering without naming the unresolved small-window roles. Keep
- * this driver inert until those roles, DART attachment and ADT-provided
- * tunables have been implemented.
+ * Stage 3 of implementing the real host-controller driver. `0034`
+ * (Stage 2: the enable sequence combined with generic ECAM enumeration,
+ * PERST left untouched) is cross-build verified and awaiting its own
+ * hardware result. This stage adds the one new variable: deasserting
+ * the external PERST# GPIO.
+ *
+ * IMPORTANT correction from the original Stage 3 draft: earlier project
+ * notes (carried over from the very first 0018 attempt's commit
+ * message) assumed pci_host_common_init() automatically deasserts
+ * PERST# for any DT child node with a reset-gpios property, via
+ * pci_host_common_parse_ports(). Reading the real pinned
+ * drivers/pci/controller/pci-host-common.c directly disproves this:
+ * pci_host_common_parse_ports() is opt-in and pci_host_common_init()
+ * never calls it. The helper only *discovers* the GPIO (via
+ * devm_fwnode_gpiod_get(..., GPIOD_ASIS, ...), which does not drive the
+ * pin); a driver must call it explicitly and then explicitly drive the
+ * discovered gpio_desc itself -- confirmed against a real in-tree
+ * caller, drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pci-imx6.c's
+ * imx_pcie_host_init()/imx_pcie_assert_perst(), which calls
+ * pci_host_common_parse_ports() once (guarded by
+ * list_empty(&bridge->ports)) and then walks bridge->ports/port->perst
+ * calling gpiod_direction_output() itself.
+ *
+ * This driver follows that same pattern: probe() calls
+ * pci_host_common_parse_ports() before pci_host_common_init() (so
+ * bridge->ports is already populated by the time .init() runs -- the
+ * helper only fetches the descriptor, it doesn't matter that this
+ * happens before the enable-sequence write). Then, at the end of
+ * .init() -- after the recovered enable sequence, matching Apple's own
+ * recovered order of "enable sequence, ..., then deassert PERST" -- it
+ * walks bridge->ports itself and calls gpiod_direction_output(desc, 0)
+ * once to deassert (GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW in the DT already encodes the
+ * polarity; logical 0 always means "not asserted" regardless of
+ * active-high/low wiring). Apple's own recovered call trace has a
+ * single deassert, not an imx6-style assert-then-deassert power
+ * sequencing dance, so this driver does the same -- no separate assert
+ * step. The post-deassert delay (PCIE_RESET_CONFIG_WAIT_MS, 100 ms) is
+ * the same generic PCIe-spec settle constant pci-host-common.h's own
+ * pci_host_common_link_train_delay() and pci-imx6.c use; it is not an
+ * Apple-recovered value, just a conservative standard margin before any
+ * bus scan can meaningfully proceed.
+ *
+ * bridge is recovered inside .init() via platform_get_drvdata(): note
+ * pci_host_common_init() itself does platform_set_drvdata(pdev, bridge)
+ * *before* it creates the ECAM window (which is what invokes .init()),
+ * so this is safe and already-set by the time .init() runs.
+ *
+ * Register offsets, bits and delays for the enable sequence itself are
+ * unchanged from `0033`/`0034` -- see those patches' own header
+ * comments and docs/plans/2026-09-13-j81-wifi-pcie.md's "Enable-sequence
+ * checkpoint" section for the full evidence trail.
  */
 
